  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 216 AM CDT Fri Sep 11 2026\n\nA surface high, centered over the Great Lakes, combined with residual \nmoisture from yesterday's rain, is leading to widespread dense fog \neast of the KC area into central/northeastern MO, as of 07Z. \nThis may eventually spread westward into parts of west central \nMO and east central KS by sunrise, given the weak pressure \ngradient and calm winds. A Dense Fog Advisory has been issued \nfor areas east of Kansas City. Will monitor trends through the \nearly morning hours. If fog does build westward, a westward \nexpansion of the Dense Fog Advisory may be needed. Fog will \ndissipate within a few hours of sunrise. \n\nFocus then shifts to the potential for strong to severe storms \nSaturday late afternoon into the overnight. Models show a shortwave \ntrough moving across Central Plains into the Midwest. This helps \npush a front southward into the area which may lead to the \ndevelopment of storms during the afternoon hours. This is uncertain \nas while there is forecast to be ample CAPE, forecast soundings show \ncapping in place as the front passes through. If storms are unable \nto develop during the day, the focus will shift to the \nevening/overnight hours, where forcing associated with the nose \nof the low-level jet will be key. Even with this, there is \nconsiderable uncertainty as the placement of the nose of the \nlow- level jet and it varies by several hundred miles in the \navailable guidance. In the deterministic guidance, the NAM moves\nthe low- level jet over the forecast area. The GFS and the \nECMWF are much further west, in central to western KS. This is \nbeyond the time from the HREF and the LREF splits the difference\nwith the low- level jet across central KS. With how far east \nthe NAM forecasts the low-level jet, it is naturally the most \nrobust solution for the forecast area, tracking storms through \nthe forecast area through the overnight hours. Conversely, the \nGFS and the ECMWF are further west, with the later showing \nlittle in the way of QPF.\n\nFor next week, the pattern becomes favorable for near daily/nightly \nprecipitation chances across the Plains and Midwest. Our forecast \narea is on the northern periphery of the upper ridging centered over \nthe Southeastern US. This effectively limits the southeastward \nextent of precipitation chances. Stronger ridging shifts the best \nchances northwestward. Weaker ridging allows for more of the \nforecast area to see higher precipitation chances. The ridging \nto the southeast will also help transport anomalously high \nmoisture northward into the region. And with the area being \npersistently in the right rear quadrant of the upper- level \njet, there is a good signal for heavy rainfall in the region, \ndespite the uncertainty in its placement. As it currently \nstands, the northern portions of the forecast have best chance \nof seeing multiple rounds of storms with potential for \ncumulatively heavy rainfall next week. Our current forecast \nshows two to perhaps five inches of rain in far northern \nMissouri next week. For the 48 hour period ending at 7 am\nThursday there is a 40-50% chance for at least 3 inches of rain.",
